---
/**
 * Method.
 *
 * Written to be read. The generator, the seed, the parameters, the controls, and — the section
 * that matters most and that most reports omit — a list of the things this document is not
 * entitled to say.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { R, OVERALL, num, pct } from '../lib/stats';
import { SEED, MONTHS, CATCHMENTS } from '../lib/generate';
---
<Base
  title="Method — The Monitoring Gap 2026"
  description="The generator, its seed and parameters, the controls, and what this report is not entitled to claim."
  chapter="Chapter 03"
  chapterTitle="Method"
>
  <section>
    <div class="page doc">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="label">Chapter 03</p>
        <h1 class="chapter">Method</h1>
        <p class="stand">
          This dataset is generated. That is not a caveat at the end of the report; it is the first
          thing on this page, because a document with this many charts in it will be quoted by
          people who did not read the page it came from.
        </p>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Each catchment is given an underlying monthly discharge rate — the events that occur,
            which nobody observes directly in the real world. Monitor coverage is simulated
            separately, month by month, with drift, noise and occasional outages where a batch of
            monitors drops out at once. The reported figure is the underlying count thinned by
            whatever was reporting.
          </p>
          <p>
            The relationship the report describes therefore emerges from the simulation rather than
            being asserted by it. We wrote a mechanism, not a finding.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div><p class="margin__k">Seed</p><span class="margin__n">{SEED}</span><p class="margin__v">mulberry32, deterministic across builds</p></div>
        <div><p class="margin__k">Period</p><span class="margin__n">{MONTHS}</span><p class="margin__v">months, Jan 2021 to Dec 2025</p></div>
        <div><p class="margin__k">Rows</p><span class="margin__n">{num(OVERALL.catchments * MONTHS)}</span><p class="margin__v">catchment-months, all on the <a class="u" href="/data">data page</a></p></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 class="head2">The control, and the draft that failed it</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The claim rests entirely on the two inputs being independent. If badly-monitored
            catchments were also the dirtiest ones, the correlation between coverage and reported
            spills would be a property of the inputs rather than of the observation, and the report
            would be circular.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the correlation between coverage and the underlying rate is computed on every build
            and published here as <span class="thread">r = {R.latentVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span>,
            against <span class="thread">r = {R.reportedVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span> for the
            reported figure. The first is the control; it is near zero, which is what it has to be.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first draft of the catchment table did not pass that check. Every badly-monitored
            catchment had also been given a high underlying rate, correlating the two at −0.87 —
            the finding typed straight into the inputs. It was caught by running the check rather
            than by anybody noticing, which is the argument for having one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Control</p>
          <span class="margin__n">{R.latentVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span>
          <p class="margin__v">coverage against underlying rate. Must be near zero</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Result</p>
          <span class="margin__n">{R.reportedVsCoverage.toFixed(2)}</span>
          <p class="margin__v">coverage against reported figure</p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc doc--wide">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 class="head2">Parameters, in full</h2>
        <table class="tbl params">
          <caption class="vh">Generator parameters per catchment.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Catchment</th><th scope="col" class="n">Overflows</th>
              <th scope="col" class="n">Rate</th><th scope="col" class="n">Coverage start</th>
              <th scope="col" class="n">Coverage end</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {CATCHMENTS.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row"><a href={`/catchments/${c.slug}`}>{c.name}</a></th>
                <td class="n" data-k="overflows">{num(c.overflows)}</td>
                <td class="n" data-k="rate">{c.latentRate.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="n" data-k="from">{pct(c.coverageStart)}</td>
                <td class="n" data-k="to">{pct(c.coverageEnd)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="note m__foot">
          Rate is discharges per overflow per month before seasonality, which is a cosine with a
          winter peak. Coverage drifts linearly between the two figures with month-to-month noise
          of ±4.5 points and a 6% monthly chance of an additional outage of 12 to 28 points.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Regenerating</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            Every figure in this report is derived at build time from these parameters and the
            seed. Nothing is stored as a rendered number.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="page doc">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 class="head2">What this report is not entitled to say</h2>
        <ol class="nots">
          <li><span class="nots__n">01</span><div><p class="nots__t">That any real river is in this condition.</p><p class="nots__b">The catchments are invented. Attaching these figures to a real catchment would be a fabrication, which is why none of them is named after one.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="nots__n">02</span><div><p class="nots__t">A national estimate of unrecorded discharges.</p><p class="nots__b">It would be the most quotable line in the document and the least defensible. The report shows coverage and lets a reader see the shape of what is missing; it does not multiply up.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="nots__n">03</span><div><p class="nots__t">Anything about the operators.</p><p class="nots__b">Operators here are labelled A to E and the assignment is arbitrary. A league table would be read as attribution and the data does not support one.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="nots__n">04</span><div><p class="nots__t">A trend.</p><p class="nots__b">The simulation holds each catchment's underlying rate flat by construction, so any apparent movement over the period is coverage. That is the point of finding 4 and also its limit.</p></div></li>
          <li><span class="nots__n">05</span><div><p class="nots__t">That the mechanism is the only one.</p><p class="nots__b">Reporting thresholds, double-counting across adjacent outfalls and the twelve-hour counting rule all affect published figures, and none of them is modelled here.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <aside class="margin">
        <div>
          <p class="margin__k">Funding</p>
          <p class="margin__v">
            A research council grant and two foundations. No operator money, no water-industry
            money, and no money from any party to a live dispute about this subject.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .m__foot { margin-top: var(--s4); max-width: 68ch; }
  .nots { border-top: 1.5px solid var(--ink); margin-top: var(--s5); }
  .nots > li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--s4);
    padding-block: var(--s4); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .nots__n { font-family: var(--sans); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
    font-size: var(--t-small); font-weight: 600; color: var(--spot); padding-top: 4px; }
  .nots__t { font-size: var(--t-body); font-weight: 600; }
  .nots__b { margin-top: 4px; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: var(--t-note); line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 58ch; }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    /* Five numeric columns will not narrow below about 400px without the coverage figures
       wrapping mid-percentage. Each catchment becomes a record with its parameters on one line. */
    .params, .params tbody, .params tr, .params td, .params th { display: block; width: 100%; }
    .params thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); }
    .params tr { padding-block: var(--s3); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .params th, .params td { border: 0; padding: 0; }
    .params th[scope='row'] { font-size: var(--t-small); font-weight: 600; }
    .params td.n { display: inline-block; text-align: left; margin-right: var(--s4); margin-top: 3px;
      font-size: var(--t-label); color: var(--ink-3); }
    .params td.n::before { content: attr(data-k) ' '; }
    .nots > li { grid-template-columns: 2.25rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--s3); }
  }
</style>
